---
title: Chickpea stew, fixed
description: Lesson 6.4. The module together. Fixing a dish one change at a time, and what to do when one has gone wrong.
servings: 3
time: 45 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, tasting-and-fixing]
lesson: "6.4"
technique: tasting and adjusting
status: draft
---

> Technique: fixing a dish one change at a time. The stew is cooked
> with no salt and no acid, on purpose. Then it is adjusted in a fixed
> order: salt, acid, fat, sweet. The order is this course's working
> convention, not a measured result; the rule that matters is one
> change, then a taste.

Heat @olive oil{2%tbsp} in a #large pot{} over medium-low heat. Add @onion{1}(finely chopped) and cook for ~{8%minutes}, until soft. Add @garlic{2%cloves}(finely chopped), @ground cumin{1%tsp} and @chilli flakes{1/4%tsp} and stir for ~{1%minute}.

Add @tinned tomatoes{400%g}, @tinned chickpeas{2%tins}(400 g each, drained and rinsed) and @water{200%ml}. Simmer for ~{20%minutes}. Stir in @spinach{100%g} until wilted, then take the pot off the heat.

Taste a spoonful. Then adjust, tasting after every step. First, add @salt{1%tsp} a pinch at a time until the stew tastes full. Second, add @lemon juice{1%tbsp} a teaspoon at a time until it tastes bright.

Third, only if it is harsh, sharp or too hot, stir in @yogurt{2%tbsp}(or more olive oil). Fourth, only if it is still sharp or bitter, add @honey{1/2%tsp}(or sugar).

> Science: in a mixture, each taste is perceived as weaker than it
> would be alone (mixture suppression), and the pairs are not
> symmetric: saltiness suppresses bitterness but not the other way
> round. That is why you change one thing and taste again: each
> addition shifts how the others read. Look up: mixture suppression,
> taste interactions.
-- source: Rebecca Hays, "Is Your Dish Too Salty? Sweet? Sour? Spicy? Here's How to Fix It.", America's Test Kitchen, 2022-07-04, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/5816-is-your-dish-too-salty-sweet-sour-spicy-here's-how-to-fix-it ; Keast, Breslin and Beauchamp, "Suppression of Bitterness Using Sodium Salts", Chimia 55(5):441, 2001, https://doi.org/10.2533/chimia.2001.441 (abstract)

> If it goes wrong: too salty, add an acid or a little sweetener, or
> stretch the dish with more unsalted food. Too sour, add fat or a
> sweetener. Too sweet, add acid, fresh herbs or a pinch of cayenne.
> Too hot, add fat or sugar. Too bitter, add salt or acid.
-- source: Rebecca Hays, "Is Your Dish Too Salty? Sweet? Sour? Spicy? Here's How to Fix It.", America's Test Kitchen, 2022-07-04, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/5816-is-your-dish-too-salty-sweet-sour-spicy-here's-how-to-fix-it ; "Seasoning Fixes", America's Test Kitchen, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/lessons/articles/2287-seasoning-fixes

> Why not a potato: the old trick of simmering a potato in the pot to
> absorb the excess was tried by a test kitchen against chilli heat,
> and it simply did not work.
-- source: Molly Birnbaum, "12 Common Cooking Myths, Debunked", America's Test Kitchen, 2019-05-30, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/1706-12-common-cooking-myths-debunked

> Science: chilli heat comes from capsaicin binding to a receptor
> channel called TRPV1. In a study of 72 people, whole milk, skimmed milk and a
> sugary soft drink reduced the burn most; skimmed milk doing as well
> as whole suggests it is not only the fat, and the mechanism is not
> settled. Look up: capsaicin, TRPV1, chemesthesis.
-- source: Yang and Zheng, "Understand spiciness: mechanism of TRPV1 channel activation by capsaicin", Protein & Cell 8(3):169-177, 2017, https://doi.org/10.1007/s13238-016-0353-7 ; Penn State, "Milk: Best drink to reduce burn from chili peppers", 2019-06-25, https://www.psu.edu/news/research/story/milk-best-drink-reduce-burn-chili-peppers (press release for a study in Physiology and Behavior)

> Why there are limits: these fixes mask a mild excess. If far too
> much salt, sugar or chilli has gone in, the damage is usually done.
> Season with a light hand while cooking and adjust at the end.
-- source: Rebecca Hays, "Is Your Dish Too Salty? Sweet? Sour? Spicy? Here's How to Fix It.", America's Test Kitchen, 2022-07-04, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/5816-is-your-dish-too-salty-sweet-sour-spicy-here's-how-to-fix-it

Serve with more yogurt on the side.

> Next time, without the recipe: taste first. Then salt, acid, fat,
> sweet, in that order, a little at a time, tasting between each. Stop
> as soon as it tastes right.
